<Base lang="en" title="Rani Laxmibai Scooty Yojana: Domicile and Income Certificate Verification by 10 November — What Students Must Check" description="Selected students under the Rani Laxmibai Scooty Yojana must get their domicile and income certificates verified by 10 November 2026 — who was selected, which certificates will be checked, and how to check your own certificate on the e-District portal." path="/en/blog/rani-laxmibai-scooty-yojana-verification-november-2026/" altPath="/blog/rani-laxmibai-scooty-yojana-verification-november-2026/" faqs={faqs} updated="8 November 2026" datePublished="2026-11-08" dateModified="2026-11-08">
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">Rani Laxmibai Scooty Yojana: domicile and income certificate verification by 10 November</h1>
<p class="note">Note: This is an independent information guide — it is not the official portal (edistrict.up.gov.in).</p>
<p class="updated-line">As per several reports (Visual Live, Primary Ka Master, CertiSetu) · 8 November 2026</p>
<figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/rani-laxmibai-scooty-yojana-verification-november-2026-featured.webp" alt="Rani Laxmibai Scooty Yojana — certificate verification (example illustration)" /><figcaption>Example illustration — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<section class="box"><h2>What has to happen by 10 November</h2><p>If you are a girl student from Uttar Pradesh who has finished graduation and has been selected under the Rani Laxmibai Scooty Yojana, the next two days matter the most. As per reports, districts have to complete verification of the domicile and income certificates of all selected students by <strong>10 November 2026</strong>. The verification will happen at the District Magistrate level and through the e-District portal — so even a small mistake in your certificate can stall the process.</p><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Details</th><th>By when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Certificate verification</td><td>Domicile and income certificates of all selected students</td><td>10 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Nodal officer</td><td>Chief Development Officer (CDO) or Additional District Magistrate (ADM) in every district</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>How it is verified</td><td>Through the e-District portal, from the District Magistrate’s login</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Scooter distribution</td><td>Announced from November — no firm date fixed yet</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">The 10 November date is the same across several independent reports, but no official order has been found in the public domain so far — confirm it with your college or district officer.</div><figure class="shot"><img src="/images/blog/scooty-verification-flow.svg" alt="Scooty Yojana certificate verification flow — certificate, e-District check, District Magistrate verification, distribution" /><figcaption>The verification flow — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Who was selected</h2><p>This scheme is for girl students of Uttar Pradesh who completed graduation in the academic session 2025-26. What is confirmed about the selection so far:</p><ul><li><strong>Only girl students:</strong> the scheme is for girls alone.</li><li><strong>UP domicile is a must:</strong> the student must be a native resident of Uttar Pradesh — which is why the domicile certificate is being checked.</li><li><strong>Top 5% merit:</strong> students in the top 5 percent of their institution’s graduating class were selected.</li><li><strong>Institutions:</strong> state and private universities, government, aided and self-financed affiliated colleges — including technical, medical, AYUSH, law and agriculture institutions.</li><li><strong>Family income up to ₹12 lakh per year:</strong> as per Government Order No. 64/2026/563 (19 August 2026), the family’s annual income must not exceed ₹12 lakh. The Higher Education Minister said the same at the Cabinet decision on 21 July 2026.</li><li><strong>Special categories:</strong> Divyang students, destitute students and daughters of martyr families get relaxation in merit — separate rules apply to their eligibility.</li></ul><p>As per reports, around 50,000 students will benefit from the scheme and the government will spend about ₹400 crore on it. The Government Order was issued on 19 August 2026 and the Cabinet approved the scheme on 21 July 2026.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Which certificates will be checked</h2><p>Verification is not limited to two certificates — the documents depend on the category of each student:</p><ul><li><strong>Domicile certificate</strong> — for every selected student. If it was not issued through e-District or its number does not show on the portal, verification can stop. See the <a href="/en/domicile-certificate/">Domicile Certificate guide</a> for documents and process.</li><li><strong>Income certificate</strong> — for every selected student. The income written in the form and the income on the certificate must not differ. See the <a href="/en/income-certificate/">Income Certificate guide</a>.</li><li><strong>Disability certificate</strong> — for Divyang students.</li><li><strong>Martyr-family dependant certificate</strong> — for daughters of martyr families.</li><li><strong>Death certificate of parents</strong> — for destitute students.</li></ul><p>Keep in mind: the verification happens on the e-District portal from the District Magistrate’s login — so your certificate number must show on the portal. If it does not, the certificate is either old, made the wrong way, or was never entered in the portal.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>How to check your own certificate</h2><p>Before waiting for the district office, check your own certificate — it takes five minutes:</p><ol><li><strong>Open edistrict.up.gov.in</strong> and choose “Praman Patra Satyapan” (certificate verification) on the homepage.</li><li><strong>Enter the application number and certificate ID</strong> — both are printed on your certificate.</li><li><strong>See the certificate shown by the portal</strong> — if it appears, it means the certificate is recorded in the e-District system.</li><li><strong>Match the name and father’s name</strong> — the name, father’s name and date of birth must be the same in Aadhaar, the university record and the certificate. Even a small spelling mistake can stop verification.</li><li><strong>Keep your phone on</strong> — the college or the district office may call or message during verification.</li></ol><p>If the certificate does not show on the portal, do not panic — the next section has the fix. The <a href="/en/status-verification/">Status and Verification guide</a> also explains how to check an application’s status in full.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>What to do if there is a mistake in the certificate</h2><p>The most common problems and their fixes:</p><ul><li><strong>The certificate number does not show on the portal:</strong> first ask your college which certificate they submitted. Then contact your district’s nodal officer (CDO or ADM) — they have been appointed as nodal officers in every district.</li><li><strong>Mistake in the name or father’s name:</strong> compare it with Aadhaar and the university record to find which document has the error. Get the wrong document corrected — if the form is right, fix the Aadhaar record; if Aadhaar is right, get the certificate corrected.</li><li><strong>Income certificate is old or wrong:</strong> schemes and scholarships need fresh income details. See the <a href="/en/income-certificate/">Income Certificate guide</a> to get a new one made — do not delay, because a new certificate takes time.</li><li><strong>Certificate was not issued through e-District:</strong> verification happens only through the e-District portal, so the certificate must be e-District-issued. If yours was made another way, ask your college and the district office at once whether it will be accepted.</li></ul><p>One thing to be clear about: students do not have to fill any online form themselves for this scheme — the universities and colleges themselves enter the data and get it verified on the NIC portal (uprlbscootyyojana.upsdc.gov.in). So if anyone asks you for money in the name of filling the form, understand that it is wrong.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>What happens after 10 November</h2><p>After verification is complete, the verified data will be sent by the District Magistrate to the vendor company that makes the scooters. Distribution has been announced from November, but no firm date is fixed yet — one report speaks of distribution after 25 November, while another says the supply work could run into January 2027. So do not trust any single news report about the date — get the correct information from your own college.</p><p>Also remember another date running in parallel: the <a href="/en/blog/up-scholarship-verification-november-2026/">UP Scholarship 2026-27 November verification schedule</a> is also under way — if you are a scholarship student too, keep the two sets of dates separate. And if you have not filled the scholarship form yet, see this too: <a href="/en/blog/up-scholarship-deadline-extension-13-november-2026/">UP Scholarship last date reported extended to 13 November — check the portal yourself first</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Beware of wrong information</h2><p>Some old and wrong information about this scheme is also circulating on the internet:</p><ul><li><strong>₹2.5 lakh income limit:</strong> this comes from an old draft of the February 2025 budget announcement. In the approved scheme the income limit is <strong>₹12 lakh</strong> — do not trust the old reports.</li><li><strong>Applying through the scholarship portal:</strong> some websites ask you to apply through scholarship.up.gov.in — that is not correct for this scheme. Here, the application happens through the university and college.</li><li><strong>Driving licence condition:</strong> one source mentions a driving licence as a requirement, but no other source has confirmed it — so do not treat it as a fixed condition.</li><li><strong>Do not pay any agent:</strong> verification is a government process — there is no agent or middleman in it.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Sources</h2><p>This article draws on: Visual Live (1 November 2026), Primary Ka Master (2 November 2026), CertiSetu (2 November 2026, citing Dainik Jagran), CSR Journal (4 November 2026), Government Order No. 64/2026/563 (19 August 2026) and the Higher Education Minister’s statement of 21 July 2026. The 10 November date is the same in several reports, but its official order is not yet available in the public domain — if the date changes, confirm from scholarship.up.gov.in or your college. NIC portal link: <a href="https://uprlbscootyyojana.upsdc.gov.in" rel="nofollow">uprlbscootyyojana.upsdc.gov.in</a>. For more guides, see the <a href="/en/">eDistrict UP homepage</a>.</p></section></div></Base>
